        <div id="how-we-ranked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How We Ranked These Powders
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these powders on label analysis, sourcing transparency, published certification status and cost per 25 g of protein at typical retail prices. We did not run our own lab tests or a tasting panel, and nothing here should be read as if we did. Where we discuss taste, foaming or mixing, we are summarizing what buyers consistently report in reviews and, in Paleovalley's and Left Coast's case, what the brands themselves say about how their powders behave.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per scoop carried the most weight, because it is the clearest signal of how concentrated a broth powder is. A tub that lists 20 g of protein in a 20 to 22 g scoop is close to pure dried broth protein. A tub that lists 13 g in a 19 g scoop either contains more non-protein solids from the broth or uses sweeteners and flavorings that take up room. Neither is dishonest, but the second costs more per gram of protein and needs a bigger scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Label simplicity came next. Every pick here has an unflavored version with no sweeteners, which is the version we recommend for most people because it works in savory and sweet recipes alike. Flavored broth powders use stevia, monk fruit or cane sugar, and some add gums for texture. None of that is harmful, but it narrows how you can use the tub and usually lowers protein per scoop.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we looked at sourcing disclosure and third-party testing. None of the five carries NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport status that we could confirm, which is typical for this category. Ancient Nutrition says its products are tested by accredited third-party labs and made in GMP-certified facilities; others make similar claims of their own. Treat brand testing claims as better than nothing, but not equivalent to an independent certification you can look up in a public database.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="amino-acids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Amino Acid Problem, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Collagen is an unusual protein. About a third of its amino acids are glycine, and much of the rest is proline and hydroxyproline, which give collagen fibers their rope-like structure. That pattern is perfect for building connective tissue and terrible for meeting essential amino acid needs. Collagen contains no tryptophan at all, one of the nine essential amino acids your body cannot make, and it is low in leucine, methionine, histidine and several others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Because of the missing tryptophan, collagen scores zero on the PDCAAS, the protein quality score regulators use. That is why collagen and many broth powder labels carry a note that the protein does not count toward the percent Daily Value for protein, or show a low percentage next to a high gram number. The grams on the label are real protein; they just are not the complete protein your body needs to build muscle on their own.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Count broth protein, but do not rely on it for muscle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">The 20 g on a bone broth label is real protein, but it is mostly collagen with no tryptophan and very little leucine. Use it to top up your day, and get your muscle-building protein from complete sources.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The research comparing collagen with whey is consistent. A 2020 trial from McMaster University in older women found that whey raised muscle protein synthesis more than the same dose of collagen. A 10-week training study in young adults, published in 2022, found that whey produced greater gains in muscle thickness than collagen even after extra leucine was added to the collagen to match whey's leucine content, although strength gains were similar. We are not aware of studies on bone broth powder specifically, but there is no reason to expect a collagen-led broth to behave differently.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this makes broth protein useless. Across a mixed diet, the missing amino acids in a collagen scoop are supplied by everything else you eat, and research funded by a collagen manufacturer suggests a substantial minority of daily protein can come from collagen without creating an essential amino acid shortfall. The practical answer is to treat a broth scoop as an add-on, not a meal's main protein. If you want help setting that main target, our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">daily protein guide</a> walks through it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Gut, Joint and Skin Claims: What the Evidence Supports
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bone broth powders are marketed heavily on gut health, joints, skin, hair and nails, and the labels often name glucosamine, chondroitin and hyaluronic acid. These compounds do occur naturally in cartilage and bone, so it is fair to say they are present. What the labels usually do not tell you is how much, and naturally occurring amounts in a 20 g scoop are not the same as the gram-level doses used in joint supplement trials. Treat those words on the label as a description of the source, not a dose.
          </p>

          <p>
            The collagen research is more useful here. Hydrolyzed collagen peptides have been studied for joint comfort and skin, and a number of randomized trials, many funded by collagen manufacturers, report modest improvements in skin elasticity and hydration and in activity-related joint discomfort over two to six months. There is also a well-known 2017 study showing that taking vitamin C-enriched gelatin before short bouts of exercise increased markers of collagen synthesis. Bone broth powder supplies similar raw material, but most of those trials used specific hydrolyzed peptides, not broth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gut health claims are the weakest. The idea that gelatin and glycine soothe or repair the gut lining is popular in wellness circles, and there is early laboratory and animal research behind it, but well-controlled human trials showing that bone broth protein improves gut conditions are lacking. If you have a diagnosed digestive condition, a powder is not a treatment, and you should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before changing your diet around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A balanced read: bone broth protein is a reasonable source of collagen-type protein, and if collagen helps your joints or skin, broth powder plausibly helps in a similar way. Claims beyond that, from immunity to detox, are marketing. This is not medical advice, and anyone with kidney disease, gout, a history of high blood pressure, pregnancy or medication questions should check with their doctor before adding a daily scoop.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fine-print" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Lead, Sodium and Label Fine Print
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bones store lead, so it is fair to ask whether concentrated bone broth carries more of it. A small 2013 study published in Medical Hypotheses found that homemade organic chicken bone broth contained several times more lead than the tap water used to make it, although the absolute amounts were still low. That single paper is not proof that commercial powders are a problem, but it is a sensible reason to prefer brands that discuss heavy-metal testing and to avoid making bone broth powder your only protein source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sodium is the other number to watch. Broth powders naturally carry some sodium even when unsalted: around 140 to 170 mg per serving on the labels we checked. That is modest, but it adds up if you drink two or three mugs a day and then season them. People on a sodium-restricted diet should read the panel and skip adding salt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Protein per scoop varies more by flavor than buyers expect. Ancient Nutrition's Pure and chocolate versions list about 20 g, but its Chicken Soup and Tomato Basil flavors list about 15 g. Left Coast's unflavored lists about 21 g while its vanilla, which contains cane sugar, lists less. Paleovalley's unflavored lists about 15 g and its flavored versions about 13 g. Always compare the exact flavor you plan to buy, not the brand's headline number.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, ignore the collagen type numbers as a buying criterion. Labels that list type I, II and III collagen are describing which tissues the collagen came from. Once it is digested into amino acids and small peptides, the type label matters far less than the dose. If you want more context on how protein powders are tested and what a contaminant certification actually covers, our guide to <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> goes deeper.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How to Use Bone Broth Protein Without Hating It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common complaint about bone broth protein is that people buy a vanilla tub, shake it with cold water like whey, and find it foamy, faintly savory and disappointing. Broth powder behaves best warm. Whisk an unflavored scoop into 8 to 12 oz of hot water, add salt, pepper, a little garlic or miso, and you have a sipping broth with roughly 15 to 21 g of protein. Most brands, including Naked and Left Coast, recommend hot liquid for exactly this reason.
          </p>

          <p>
            In cooking, unflavored broth powder disappears into soups, stews, chili, gravies and sauces, adding protein without changing the dish much. Stir it into the liquid near the end rather than boiling it hard for an hour, and add it gradually to avoid clumps. It also works in mashed potatoes, savory oatmeal and rice cooked in broth. This is where bone broth protein beats every other powder: whey and plant proteins taste wrong in savory food, while broth protein tastes like it belongs.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cold use is possible but takes technique. A blender handles it far better than a shaker, and Left Coast advises adding the powder last and blending on low to limit foaming. Chocolate and vanilla broth powders are designed for smoothies, but buyers frequently report that they taste less dessert-like than whey. Pair them with banana, nut butter or cocoa rather than water alone.
          </p>

          <p>
            Timing matters less than total intake. There is no special window for broth protein. Some people like a mug in the afternoon as a low-calorie snack, others add a scoop to dinner. If you train, keep a complete protein such as whey or a well-built plant blend as your main post-workout source; our <a href="/when-to-take-protein-powder/">guide to when to take protein powder</a> explains why total daily protein beats timing for most people.
          </p>
        </div>

