        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Bone Broth Protein, and Who Should Skip It
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy it if you avoid dairy, prefer animal protein over plant protein, and like savory food. Bone broth powder is the only protein powder that genuinely improves a bowl of soup, and an unflavored tub from Ancient Nutrition, Left Coast or Naked gives you 20 g or so of protein per scoop for about 90 calories. It is also a sensible choice for people following paleo or carnivore-style diets who want a convenient, minimally processed protein add-in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy it, with realistic expectations, if your interest is joints, skin or hair. Bone broth protein supplies the same kind of collagen raw material studied in those trials, and some people prefer a whole-food style powder to refined collagen peptides. Just know that the strongest evidence is for specific hydrolyzed collagen products, and that broth powder costs more per gram than plain collagen.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it, or at least do not rely on it, if your main goal is building muscle or preserving it while dieting. A complete protein with plenty of leucine does that job better per gram and per dollar. Whey isolate is the most efficient option for most people who tolerate dairy; see our <a href="/best-whey-protein-isolate/">best whey protein isolate</a> guide. If you are dairy-free, beef protein isolate, egg white protein or a pea and rice blend will do more for muscle than broth.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip it as well if price per gram is your main filter, or if you dislike the idea of a savory protein drink. And if you are already leaning toward Paleovalley for its grass-fed, bone-only sourcing, our full <a href="/paleovalley-bone-broth-protein-review/">Paleovalley bone broth protein review</a> covers each flavor, the protein-per-scoop trade-off and whether the premium is worth it for your use.
          </p>
        </div>

