              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating It Like Whey for Muscle</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bone broth protein is mostly collagen, with no tryptophan and very little leucine. Twenty grams of it will not do what 20 g of whey does for muscle protein synthesis. Use it to top up your daily protein, and keep a complete protein such as whey, egg, beef isolate or a plant blend as your main post-workout source.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Brands on the Headline Number</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The protein figure on the front of the tub is often for the brand's best flavor. Savory and sweetened versions frequently carry less, sometimes 13 to 15 g rather than 20 g. Compare the Nutrition Facts panel for the exact flavor and size you plan to buy before deciding which brand is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking It Cold in Water</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Broth powder is designed to behave like broth. Shaken cold in plain water it can taste flat and foam, which is why many disappointed buyers give up on it. Whisk the unflavored version into hot water with salt and herbs, cook with it, or blend the flavored versions into a smoothie with real ingredients.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Flavored Tubs for Cooking</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Vanilla and chocolate broth powders are sweetened with stevia, monk fruit or sugar, which ruins them for soup, gravy and savory recipes. If you plan to cook with it, or are not sure how you will use it, buy the unflavored version first. It is usually the most protein-dense and the most flexible tub in the range.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking Joint Claims as Doses</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Labels that name glucosamine, chondroitin or hyaluronic acid are describing compounds naturally present in cartilage, not clinical doses. Do not drop a prescribed treatment or dedicated joint supplement on the strength of a broth powder label. If joint pain is the reason you are buying, discuss it with a doctor or physical therapist.
              </p>
