              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is bone broth protein a complete protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The protein in bone broth powder is mostly collagen, which contains no tryptophan and is low in leucine and several other essential amino acids. That gives it a protein quality score of zero on the PDCAAS scale. The grams are real, but they should supplement complete proteins like meat, eggs, dairy or a good plant blend rather than replace them.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is bone broth protein good for building muscle?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not on its own. Studies comparing collagen with whey consistently find whey stimulates more muscle protein synthesis and larger muscle gains, even when extra leucine is added to the collagen. Bone broth protein is collagen-led, so the same logic applies. It can contribute to total daily protein, but a complete protein is the better choice after training.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between bone broth protein and collagen peptides?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Both are mostly collagen. Collagen peptides are hydrolyzed into small fragments, usually from hides or fish skin, and dissolve almost invisibly in coffee. Bone broth protein is concentrated, dried broth from bones and connective tissue, so it tastes more savory and mixes less smoothly. Nutritionally they are similar, and plain collagen is usually cheaper per gram.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much protein is in a scoop of bone broth protein?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most unflavored bone broth powders list 19 to 21 g of protein per serving, per their labels at the time of writing. Ancient Nutrition Pure lists about 20 g, Left Coast about 21 g and Nutricost about 19 g. Some brands and flavors are lower: Paleovalley lists about 13 to 15 g, and savory soup flavors often around 15 g.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is bone broth protein powder dairy-free?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Bone broth protein is made from animal bones and connective tissue, not milk, so it is naturally dairy-free and lactose-free. That makes it useful for people with a dairy intolerance who still want animal protein. Check the label for any added ingredients in flavored versions, and anyone with a severe allergy should confirm manufacturing practices with the brand.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does bone broth powder contain lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Bones store lead, and one small 2013 study found homemade chicken bone broth had several times more lead than the water used to make it, though the absolute amounts were low. That is not proof that commercial powders are unsafe, but it is a reason to choose brands that discuss heavy-metal testing and to avoid relying on broth as your only protein source.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I cook with bone broth protein powder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is the best way to use it. Unflavored broth powder blends into soups, stews, chili, sauces, gravies and rice dishes, adding protein without changing the flavor much. Stir it into warm liquid gradually near the end of cooking to avoid clumps. Flavored versions are sweetened, so keep those for smoothies and baking rather than savory recipes.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
